 ## Overview
-Adaptive Step Size Control is a family of optimization techniques in volume rendering and distance-field ray marching that dynamically adjusts the traversal step size $\Delta t$ based on local scene properties, such as density, distance from the camera, or statistical variance. It solves the performance-quality trade-off inherent in fixed-step sampling by using fine steps in high-detail or high-density regions and coarse steps in empty or homogeneous areas [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. Unlike its siblings, this family encompasses methods ranging from noise-driven temporal accumulation [2312-adaptive-step-size-ray-marching.md] and exponential empty-space skipping [2442-dynamic-sampling-step-adjustment.md] to preprocessing-based variance mapping [13161-variance-based-step-size-adaptation.md].
+Adaptive Step Size Control is a family of optimization techniques for distance-field ray marching and volume rendering that dynamically adjusts the sampling interval $\Delta t$ along a ray $r(t) = \mathbf{o} + t\mathbf{d}$ to balance rendering quality and computational cost [Parent]. Unlike fixed-step methods that use a uniform interval regardless of scene content, this family varies $\Delta t$ based on local density, geometric complexity, or user interaction state to skip empty regions or reduce samples in low-detail areas [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Safe Raymarching Step Size](1462-safe-raymarching-step-size.md) by prioritizing performance adaptation over strict geometric safety guarantees, and from [Error Estimate](2768-error-estimate.md) by using heuristic or precomputed metrics rather than runtime error bounds to drive step selection.
 
