 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive step sizing in volume rendering emerged to address the high computational cost of fixed-step ray marching, particularly for large datasets. Early work by Hadwiger and Rezk Salama (2004) proposed increasing step sizes during interactive viewpoint changes to maintain frame rates, followed by high-quality rendering for still images [S2, unofficial]. Later developments, such as those by Weiskopf et al. (TVCG 2003), explored empty space skipping and homogeneity acceleration [S2, unofficial]. More recent implementations leverage GPU architectures and deep learning frameworks, such as the PyTorch-based adaptive sampling and reconstruction methods [S1, unofficial], to handle 4D volumes and temporal accumulation with noise-driven step adjustments [S2, S3, unofficial].
+The concept of adaptive sampling in volume rendering emerged to address the high computational cost of uniform ray marching, particularly for large datasets or real-time applications. Early work focused on precomputing density statistics to guide step sizes [S1, unofficial]. The specific implementation of noise-driven adaptive step size with temporal accumulation for 4D volumes was developed in the context of the "AdaptiveSampling" project [S1, unofficial]. The PyTorch-based "Adaptive Stepsize Rendering" variant was also part of this ecosystem, focusing on differentiable rendering pipelines [S1, unofficial]. These methods are primarily used in real-time visualization, medical imaging, and scientific visualization where interactive frame rates are required.
 
